Footnote 23 of FinCEN’s October 5 proposed rule states:

> “PSB pledged its ownership stake in A7 LLC to VEB as collateral for loans to A7 LLC.” [Federal Register](https://www.federalregister.gov/public-inspection/2026-20371/special-measure-prohibiting-the-transmittal-of-funds-regarding-transactions-involving-the-a7)

That describes a **secured financing relationship**: VEB lent to A7, with the ownership interest held by Promsvyazbank—PSB—serving as collateral. It does not describe VEB merely encountering an A7 transaction somewhere in the banking system.

I followed FinCEN’s citation back to the Centre for Information Resilience’s **October 2025 *A7 Abroad* investigation** and inspected its corporate-structure diagram. That report dates the pledge arrangement to February 2025 and identifies PSB’s stake as 49%, alongside Ilan Shor’s 51%. It also reports a separate company, **Exim International**, owned 75% by Shor and 25% by VEB, which advertised itself as part of the A7 group. [InfoRes](https://www.info-res.org/app/uploads/2025/10/A7-Abroad-FINAL-Copy.pdf)

There is an important sourcing distinction: **FinCEN cites that investigation; these are not two independent discoveries of the same loan.** The report relies on corporate information and financial reporting. I have not obtained the executed loan agreement or the complete pledge instrument.

Nevertheless, this is an explicit financing relationship in a published official record.

